    "created_at": "2019-04-18T02:08:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-04-18T02:08:35Z",
    "body": "Personally, I'm used to and love YAML. It's easy-peasy to write, looks great and works even when you write bad YAML.\r\n\r\nI can see how TOML can be easier to parse and it is reminiscent of shell variables. The nesting doesn't look very nice though and the main syntax delimiters are in awkward far-reaching locations.\r\n\r\nI'd be happy with either YAML or TOML, I'd suggest maybe allowing the user to decide which configuration format they prefer and automatically use the correct parser? We could do this based on file extension or a simple syntax check.\r\n\r\nI don't think JSON is appropriate for human-facing configuration files and I'd vote that off the table, if we're voting. JSON is for 🤖.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
